SCIM -- Smart Common Input Method
=================================

SCIM is a modular input-method platform.  A single backend hosts the IMEngine
modules (pinyin, table, rawcode, ...); frontends expose it to applications, a
candidate renderer shows the preedit and candidate list at the cursor, and a
panel provides the toolbar and status area.

Source-derived developer documentation is generated with doxygen.  Look for
scim-doc-$(VERSION).tar.gz on our website or browse it online.

Building
========

To build from VCS, run the bootstrap script in the top-level directory first:

  ./bootstrap && ./configure && make

To prepare a release tarball, adjust AC_INIT, SCIM_*_VERSION, SCIM_CURRENT and
SCIM_REVISION in configure.ac as needed, then run

  ./bootstrap && ./configure && make dist && make docs

The GUI (panel and setup) targets GTK4.  Native IM modules are built for GTK2,
GTK3, GTK4, Qt5 and Qt6 when the respective toolkits are present.


Running scim
============

  scim [option]...

  The options are:
    -l, --list            List all of the available modules.
    -f, --frontend name   Use the specified FrontEnd module (e.g. x11, socket,
                          wayland, ibus); auto-detected from the session when
                          not given.
    -c, --config name     Use the specified Config module.
    -e, --engines name    Load the specified set of IMEngines.
    -ne,--no-engines name Do not load that set of IMEngines.
    -d, --daemon          Run in the background as a supervisor (restarts its
                          children; on a GNOME/IBus session it runs the
                          input-source coordinator instead of a frontend).
    --no-socket           Do not try to start a SCIM SocketFrontEnd daemon.
    --xml                 With '-f ibus' (after '--'), print the IBus component
                          manifest for the installed engines and exit.
    -h, --help            Show this help message.

When no frontend is given, scim picks one from the session: a GNOME/IBus
session is served by the IBus frontend (see below), a plain X11 session by the
x11 frontend, and a Wayland session by the wayland frontend when it is built.

`scim -d` daemonizes itself and stays as a persistent supervisor: it launches
the appropriate worker(s) and restarts them if they crash.  Install
data/scim.desktop as an XDG autostart entry (the build does this) so the
session starts `scim -d` automatically; scim then self-selects its role.


Client/server architecture (Socket FrontEnd)
============================================

By default scim runs a single per-user client/server environment.  One process
-- the SocketFrontEnd daemon -- owns the real backend and the config files;
everything else is a thin client that relays to it over a local socket:

  * the GTK2/3/4 and Qt5/6 IM modules load the Socket IMEngine + Socket Config
    proxies and relay key processing AND configuration to the daemon (they
    embed the backend directly only as a fallback -- e.g. no socket module
    built, manual mode, or a mismatched socket address);
  * the x11, wayland and ibus frontends do the same when a daemon is present.

So there is exactly one process reading the config files and running the
engines; activation is instant and learned state is shared.  Pass --no-socket
(or -c/-e explicitly) to opt out and embed the backend in-process instead.

The default socket address is local:$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/scim-socket-frontend (and
local:/tmp/scim-socket-frontend when XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is not set); the local:
prefix means a unix socket.  Use an inet: prefix for a TCP socket, e.g.
SCIM_SOCKET_ADDRESS=inet:localhost:12345.

The IM modules use the Socket IMEngine/Config modules by default.  To load
other modules directly instead, set for example:

  $ export GTK_IM_SCIM_CONFIG_MODULE=simple
  $ export GTK_IM_SCIM_IMENGINE_MODULES=table


GNOME / IBus sessions
=====================

On a GNOME (or any IBus) session the IME is served by ibus-daemon through the
scim IBus engine, not by our own input frontend.  Here `scim -d` runs a single
supervised daemon that hosts one shared backend for two co-loaded frontends:

  scim -f socket,ibussync

  * socket   -- a warm SocketFrontEnd: the shared backend the relay-mode IBus
                engine attaches to, so activating an engine is instant and
                learned state survives ibus-daemon killing and relaunching the
                engine process;
  * ibussync -- a coordinator that keeps the set of enabled SCIM engines and
                GNOME's input-source list (org.gnome.desktop.input-sources) in
                sync, both directions, off that same backend.

IBus discovers SCIM engines from a component manifest (scim.xml); see below.


IBus component manifest (scim.xml)
==================================

NOTE: This whole section applies to GNOME/IBus sessions ONLY.  On a plain X11
or Wayland session the x11/wayland frontend hosts the backend directly and
discovers engines from it, so no IBus component manifest is involved -- neither
packagers nor users need to generate or install scim.xml there.

ibus-daemon reads a component manifest to learn which engines exist and how to
launch them.  SCIM installs a bootstrap manifest to the IBus component
directory (`pkg-config --variable=pkgdatadir ibus-1.0`/component, typically
/usr/share/ibus/component/scim.xml) that lists only the generic "scim" engine.

To expose every *installed* SCIM engine as its own IBus entry, regenerate the
manifest:

  scim -f ibus -- --xml > /usr/share/ibus/component/scim.xml

This lists all installed engines, enabled or not: the manifest is the set of
engines GNOME/IBus can OFFER, and which of them are actually active is a
separate dimension the ibussync coordinator keeps in sync with GNOME.  So you
regenerate only when engines are INSTALLED or removed, not when you enable or
disable one.  The generator enumerates the engines visible in the environment
it runs in, so run it as the identity whose engines you want to appear.
ibus-daemon scans the component directories once at startup, so restart
ibus-daemon afterwards (e.g. `ibus restart`).

For packagers
-------------

Regenerate the system manifest whenever the set of installed SCIM IMEngine
packages changes -- i.e. watch the IMEngine module directory
(`$(libdir)/scim-1.0/*/IMEngine/`) and, on any install or removal, run

  scim -f ibus -- --xml > /usr/share/ibus/component/scim.xml

Wire this to your package manager's hooks: a dpkg trigger, an rpm file trigger,
or a pacman hook on that directory.  Run it as root, so it enumerates only the
system-wide engines -- exactly what belongs in the system manifest.  (Tables
and other engines that live only in a user's home are handled per-user below.)

For users -- exposing a user-level engine
-----------------------------------------

An engine that lives only in your home directory -- e.g. a scim-tables table
you installed for your user, or a licensed engine that cannot be installed
system-wide -- is invisible to the system generator.  To use it under IBus,
generate your own manifest and add its directory to ibus-daemon's search path:

  1. Generate a personal manifest as yourself.  Run as you, the generator sees
     both the system-wide engines AND the ones in your home, so it lists every
     installed engine from both (enabled or not):

       mkdir -p ~/.local/share/ibus/component
       scim -f ibus -- --xml > ~/.local/share/ibus/component/scim.xml

  2. Point IBUS_COMPONENT_PATH at BOTH your directory AND the system component
     directory, in a way that reaches ibus-daemon.  On modern desktops
     ibus-daemon runs as a systemd USER service (org.freedesktop.IBus.session.
     *.service), so it inherits the systemd user manager's environment, NOT your
     login shell profile -- setting the variable in ~/.bashrc or ~/.profile does
     NOT reach it.  Set it in the systemd user environment instead, via
     ~/.config/environment.d/ (read by systemd --user at login, before it starts
     ibus-daemon).  Find your system component dir once:

       pkg-config --variable=pkgdatadir ibus-1.0     # e.g. /usr/share/ibus

     then create ~/.config/environment.d/scim-ibus.conf with the resolved paths
     (environment.d does no command substitution; ${HOME} is fine):

       IBUS_COMPONENT_PATH=${HOME}/.local/share/ibus/component:/usr/share/ibus/component

  3. Log out and back in so systemd --user re-reads environment.d and restarts
     ibus-daemon with the new value (a plain `ibus restart` may not pick it up,
     since it keeps the old environment).

     (Sessions that still start ibus-daemon from an X startup script rather than
     systemd read ~/.xprofile; a system-wide alternative that reaches any
     session is /etc/environment, but it needs root and affects all users.)

IMPORTANT: IBUS_COMPONENT_PATH REPLACES ibus-daemon's default search path
rather than adding to it, and ibus-daemon does not scan any per-user directory
by default.  So you must list the system directory too, or ibus-daemon will
stop seeing every other component installed there (the system SCIM manifest and
any other input methods).  ibus-daemon merges the manifests it finds: engines
from all scanned files are UNIONed by engine name (on a duplicate name the
first one loaded wins), so your personal manifest and the system one coexist --
your home-only engines simply add to the system set.  Regenerate your manifest
whenever you install or remove a user-level engine.

Enable vs. install: the manifest lists every installed engine, so you do NOT
regenerate it when you enable or disable an engine.  Enabling/disabling is the
"active" dimension -- the ibussync coordinator keeps SCIM's enabled set and
GNOME's input-source list in sync, both directions -- while the manifest is the
"available" dimension that only changes when engines are installed or removed.
Because a user-installed engine now appears in the manifest even while disabled,
you can add it straight from GNOME's input-source chooser.


Tips
====

* The candidate window (preedit and candidate list) has its own appearance,
  configured under /Candidates/Default/* -- scim-setup exposes the font
  ("Candidate font") and the four colors ("Candidate colors").  Each setting
  falls back to the panel's /Panel/Gtk/* value until you give it a
  candidate-specific value (e.g. a larger candidate font), so existing setups
  look unchanged.  Changes are applied on the fly, without restarting.
* Panel appearance (font/colors of the toolbar and status area) is configured
  in scim-setup; changes are pushed to running clients and applied on the fly.
* The panel's toolbar window -- and, when the panel draws candidates itself
  (built without the candidate renderer), its input and lookup windows -- can be
  dragged with the mouse.
